    private Map<String, DataDestination> destinations = new HashMap<>();
    private ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
    private BlockingQueue<DataRecord> dataQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

    public void addDataSource(String name, DataSource source) {
        sources.put(name, source);
    }

    public void addDataDestination(String name, DataDestination destination) {
        destinations.put(name, destination);
    }

    public void startPipeline() {
        for (DataSource source : sources.values()) {
            executorService.submit(() -> processSource(source));
        }
        executorService.submit(this::processQueue);
    }

    private void processSource(DataSource source) {
        try {
            DataRecord record = source.fetch();
            while (record != null) {
                dataQueue.put(record);
                record = source.fetch();
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    private void processQueue() {
        try {
            while (true) {
                DataRecord record = dataQueue.take();
                for (DataDestination destination : destinations.values()) {
                    destination.deliver(record);
                }
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    public abstract void shutdown();

    public interface DataSource {
        DataRecord fetch() throws InterruptedException;
    }

    public interface DataDestination {
        void deliver(DataRecord record);
    }

    public static class DataRecord {
        // Define the structure of a data record
    }
}
```

This code provides a basic framework for a `DataPipelineManager` class in Java. It includes methods for adding data sources and destinations, starting the pipeline, and processing data records asynchronously. The pipeline uses a blocking queue to decouple data ingestion from delivery, ensuring that data is processed as soon as it becomes available.

The class is designed to be extensible, allowing new data sources, transformations, and delivery destinations to be easily added by implementing the `DataSource` and `DataDestination` interfaces. This design follows principles of modularity and separation of concerns, making it easier to maintain and scale the system over time.

Please note that this code is a simplified version and does not include all the features outlined in the requirements, such as real-time processing, event-driven notifications, transactional processing, thread safety, caching, resource optimization, monitoring, and logging. These features would require additional implementation to fully meet the specified requirements.']

